  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1137 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nAs per the pattern, the synoptic structure aloft remains dominated \nby a strong high pressure system over the central CONUS. Nearer to \nthe surface, a weak low pressure system is in place over Colorado. \nEarlier this evening some very light showers popped over various \nboundaries, but have since dissolved. There is widespread convection \nmoving into eastern Colorado that threatens the CWA later tonight. \nThe CAMs have some of this convection entering Kansas around 1 AM. \nThere is model disagreement on exactly where and how much coverage \nthis convection with have in Kansas. The moisture is available, but \nthe CAPE values forecast are significantly lacking in terms of \nstrong convection. At this time, a severe threat it not \nanticipated. The only threats forecast are lightning and perhaps \nlocalized flooding. \n\nTomorrow is forecast to be the hottest day of the forecast period \nwith highs around 100-105 degrees. A heat advisory is in effect from \n1-9 PM for Meade, Seward, Barber, Comanche, and Clark counties for \nheat indices as high as 110 degrees. The traditional heat \nprecautions are strongly advised. In the previous days, CAMs have \nhad relative agreement in an MCS coming out of Nebraska late on \nThursday and clipping our northeastern zones. This is now either \ngone, or pushed way eastward. Some CAMs have some isolated \nconvection initiating in the vacuum near those same zones, but there \nis almost no agreement. Similarly, some CAMs have some potentially \nsevere convection in far SW Kansas. All this to say there is high \nuncertainty on marginally severe convection, especially pending the \novernight storms. Forecasted soundings have very marginal CAPE and \nshear values that really put a pause on talking up the severe \nthreat. As such, it is more of a wait and see day with a situation \nthat may rapidly evolve underneath a plethora of uncertainty. \n\nThere is a smaller pattern shift beyond Thursday with marginally \ncooler temperatures with drier weather. Highs in the mid-to-upper \n90s with no forecasted precipitation chances. Nothing out of the \nordinary for SW Kansas in early August.",
